DSH-Android patch 24: a proxy that refuses connections is skipped (dsh-http-proxy), and the address a
transport failure actually hit is named (vendored @snnh/dsh-llm-service-lite 0.2.1). Applies on top
of 15 (dsh-http-proxy) and 14, 16, 17, 18, 22, 23 (llm-service-lite).

The app hands the engine Android's proxy for the default network (EngineProxy). Android's own HTTP
stack treats that proxy as a preference: when it refuses the connection, it connects directly. undici's
ProxyAgent has no fallback. A capture or VPN app's local proxy (Reqable, HttpCanary: 127.0.0.1:port)
left in the Wi-Fi settings after the app stopped therefore failed every engine request with
ECONNREFUSED while the app's model test, through HttpURLConnection, passed (#75); and the message
named the endpoint (`ECONNREFUSED ollama.com:443`), not the dead proxy. Now:
 - dsh-http-proxy's global dispatcher checks that a proxy accepts TCP connections (≤ 1.5 s, the
   answer trusted for 30 s) before routing through it, and connects directly while it does not;
   transitions are logged without credentials;
 - llm-service-lite's transport cause appends the address that failed when it is not the endpoint's
   own: `ECONNREFUSED ollama.com:443 via proxy 127.0.0.1:9000 (connection refused)`, or
   `… at <resolved address>` (e.g. a DNS answer of 127.0.0.1).

